# Overview

Full-Stack + LLM Service Developer
Unified Action Guidelines (Debug-First, Maintainability-First)
1. Core Engineering Philosophy
Example
# Good
def generate_answer(prompt: str) -> str:
    return llm_client.run(prompt)

# Bad
def generate_answer(prompt):
    return model.generate(prompt, temperature=0.7, max_tokens=512)

General Rule

Code should explain intent, not implementation details.

Performance optimization happens after profiling, not before.

Action Guidelines

Prefer clarity over cleverness

Prefer predictable behavior over micro-optimizations

Treat debugging time as the most expensive resource

2. Backend Architecture (FastAPI)
Example (Minimal & Clean)
# api/chat.py
@router.post("/chat")
async def chat(req: ChatRequest):
    return chat_service.generate(req)

# services/chat_service.py
def generate(req: ChatRequest):
    prompt = prompt_builder.build(req)
    return llm_client.run(prompt)

General Rule

API layer = HTTP only

Service layer = business logic

No computation inside routers

Action Guidelines

One file, one responsibility

Routers must remain thin

Services must be testable without FastAPI

3. LLM Serving Principles
Example
class LLMClient:
    def run(self, prompt: str) -> str:
        return self.model.generate(prompt)

General Rule

LLMs are replaceable infrastructure, not core logic.

Action Guidelines

Always wrap models with a client class

Never call the model directly from routers

Assume future model replacement (OpenAI → Local → vLLM)

4. Database Usage Rules
Example
def get_user(db: Session, user_id: int):
    return db.query(User).filter(User.id == user_id).first()

General Rule

Queries must be readable without documentation.

Action Guidelines

Avoid ORM magic

Keep feature data separate from transactional data

Use repository pattern for complex queries

5. Frontend / Backend Separation
Example
<!-- html/chat.html -->
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/chat.css">
<script src="/js/chat.js"></script>

// js/chat.js
async function sendMessage() {
  await fetch("/api/chat")
}

General Rule

HTML = structure

CSS = appearance

JS = behavior

Action Guidelines

No HTML generation inside JS

No logic inside CSS

Backend returns JSON only

6. Debug-First Development Strategy
Example
logger.info(
    "chat_request_received",
    extra={"user_id": req.user_id}
)

General Rule

If you cannot debug it in production logs, it is not ready.

Action Guidelines

Log all external I/O (DB, LLM, APIs)

Avoid silent exceptions

Prefer explicit errors over defensive try/except

7. ML / DL Knowledge for Service Engineers
Example

Long input → context window overflow → response degradation

General Rule

You do not need to train models,
but you must understand model behavior limits.

Action Guidelines

Understand:

Overfitting vs Generalization

Tokens and context windows

Latency vs cost tradeoffs

Implement:

Chunking

Summarization

Caching

8. Performance Philosophy
Example
Slow but debuggable > Fast but opaque

General Rule

Measure before optimizing.

Action Guidelines

Add profiling only when needed

Never optimize speculative bottlenecks

Cache only after correctness is guaranteed

9. Project Structure (Recommended)
backend/
 ├─ main.py
 ├─ api/
 ├─ services/
 ├─ models/
 ├─ db/
 ├─ clients/
 └─ utils/

frontend/
 ├─ html/
 ├─ css/
 └─ js/

10. Final Engineering Checklist

 Can I trace a request end-to-end via logs?

 Can I replace the LLM without rewriting logic?

 Are frontend and backend completely decoupled?

 Can a new developer understand this in one hour?

 Is debugging easier than optimization?
